<article class="learn">
  <header class="learn__head">
    <p class="learn__kicker">Learn</p>
    <h1>How your content reaches a model, and what changes when it does</h1>
    <p class="learn__lead">
      Two separate systems decide whether an LLM knows about your product. One built the model
      months ago and cannot be changed. The other answers a question live, right now, and can be
      changed this afternoon. Most advice about "AI visibility" mixes them up, so it points at the
      wrong lever.
    </p>
    <p class="learn__meta">
      This page is the model behind <a href="/test">the four checks on /test</a>. The measurements
      and sources are in <a href="/guide">the full guide</a>.
    </p>
  </header>

  <nav class="learn__toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#two-systems">Training and retrieval are different systems</a></li>
      <li><a href="#training">Training: collected first, filtered second</a></li>
      <li><a href="#retrieval">Retrieval: what an agent can fetch today</a></li>
      <li><a href="#outcomes">What the four outcomes mean for you</a></li>
      <li><a href="#glossary">Glossary</a></li>
      <li><a href="#limits">What this cannot tell you</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <%# --- 1. the framing ------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <section id="two-systems">
    <h2>Training and retrieval are different systems with different gates</h2>

    <p>
      A model can quote your documentation for two unrelated reasons. Either your text was in the
      corpus it was trained on, so the phrasing is inside the weights. Or an agent fetched your page
      seconds ago because someone asked a question. The two run on different infrastructure, respond
      to different signals, and fail in different ways.
    </p>

    <div class="learn__split">
      <div class="learn__col">
        <h3>Training</h3>
        <p class="learn__colmeta">Slow, one-way, already decided</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Content is <strong>collected</strong> by a web crawler or from public code hosting.</li>
          <li>What survives is then <strong>filtered</strong> for quality, license and duplication.</li>
          <li>You cannot remove yourself from a corpus that already shipped.</li>
          <li>You cannot correct a version number a model learned two years ago.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="learn__col">
        <h3>Retrieval</h3>
        <p class="learn__colmeta">Live, per question, fixable today</p>
        <ul>
          <li>An agent fetches a URL while answering, or reads a search index built last week.</li>
          <li><code>robots.txt</code>, your WAF and your markup decide what it gets.</li>
          <li>A change you deploy today takes effect on the next question.</li>
          <li>It only happens when something points the agent at you.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="learn__note">
      Both matter, and neither substitutes for the other. Being in the training data makes you the
      default answer when nobody is looking anything up. Being retrievable is what keeps that answer
      current.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- 2. training ---------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <section id="training">
    <h2>Training: content is collected first, then filtered</h2>

    <p>
      Text has to pass two gates to reach a training corpus. It has to be <strong>collected</strong>,
      and then it has to <strong>survive the corpus builder's filter</strong>. Being public and
      crawlable clears the first gate only. Modern web pipelines discard roughly 90% of what they
      collect (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.01116" rel="noopener">RefinedWeb</a>).
    </p>

    <p>
      There are two routes through those gates, and they behave so differently that the same page can
      pass on one and fail on the other.
    </p>

    <h3 id="web-channel">The web channel collects a sample and then discards most of it</h3>
    <p>
      Open web corpora start from <a href="#common-crawl">Common Crawl</a>. Its crawler ranks domains
      by harmonic centrality, gives higher-ranked domains a larger crawl budget, and
      <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3630106.3659033" rel="noopener">never copies any domain
      in full</a>. Being online is not the same as being crawled. New, deep, weakly linked pages are
      the ones it misses, which is why a sitemap and internal links matter here and nowhere else.
    </p>
    <p>
      We measured this on two sites we control or track: Common Crawl held 37% of evilmartians.com
      (221 of 600 pages) and 27% of docs.anycable.io (34 of 125). Crawled and missed pages scored the
      same on quality, so the gap comes entirely from how the crawler samples links.
    </p>

    <h3 id="quality-filter">The quality filter was tuned away from technical reference</h3>
    <p>
      What Common Crawl collects then meets a classifier. The open one, and the proxy we score
      against, is <a href="#fineweb-edu">FineWeb-Edu</a>: it keeps a document when its score rounds
      to 3 or higher (raw 2.5), and it was
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.17557" rel="noopener">deliberately tuned toward
      school-level explanation and away from technical pages</a>. Reference documentation,
      configuration tables and API pages fail it almost without exception. Of 118 pages on
      docs.anycable.io, zero cleared the bar. Of 598 pages on evilmartians.com, five did.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only the first ~512 tokens are scored, so the opening decides the whole document. In our
      rewrite experiment, stripping boilerplate moved a blog post from 2.43 to 2.48 and made a
      reference page worse. Rewriting the lead so it explains what the reader will learn took the
      same blog post to 3.19. The reference page never cleared the bar, even fully rewritten. Genre
      sets the ceiling.
    </p>

    <h3 id="code-channel">The code channel skips the prose filter entirely</h3>
    <p>
      Code corpora take files from public repositories with no prose-quality classifier at all. That
      is the single most important asymmetry on this page: the Markdown in your repo faces a license
      check and a duplicate check, and nothing else. A reference page that scores 1.6 on the web
      filter is collected without complaint when it lives in a public repo.
    </p>
    <p>
      The route has three steps, and teams usually only know about the first one:
    </p>
    <ol class="learn__steps">
      <li>
        <strong>The repo is public on GitHub.</strong> Private content never enters a code corpus.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong id="software-heritage">Software Heritage has archived it.</strong>
        <a href="#the-stack">The Stack v2 and v3</a> are built from the
        <a href="https://archive.softwareheritage.org/" rel="noopener">Software Heritage</a> archive,
        and archival is a separate step that is neither automatic nor star-driven. A permissive
        license on an unarchived repo buys nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong id="licenses">The license is not disqualifying.</strong> The Stack v2 and v3 keep
        files labelled <code>permissive</code> <em>and</em> <code>no_license</code>, and drop
        <code>non_permissive</code>: GPL and similar, "all rights reserved", and source-available
        licenses such as BUSL, FSL and SSPL. The Stack v1 dropped unlicensed files. That rule is
        three dataset generations old and is still repeated as current advice.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="learn__note">
      GitHub's API reports <code>NOASSERTION</code> for every source-available license, so a
      dashboard reading <code>spdx_id</code> cannot tell an Elastic-licensed repo from an unlicensed
      weekend project. Our check reads the LICENSE text instead.
    </p>

    <h3 id="copies">Copies predict memorization; stars do not</h3>
    <p>
      We probed 168 documentation repositories and 309 passages. Stars correlated with verbatim
      recall at ρ = +0.006 (p = 0.94), which is nothing. <code>vercel/next.js</code> (141k stars) and
      <code>supabase/supabase</code> (107k stars) produced no verbatim runs at all, while
      <code>getsurfboard/manual</code> (115 stars) produced a 33-word one. In a denser study of 15
      repositories, the number of independent copies of a passage correlated at ρ = +0.70
      (p = 0.005).
    </p>
    <p>
      Stars measure how many people liked a project. Copies measure how many times its sentences
      exist in the world, and a corpus counts the second one. Deduplication is the reason: near
      duplicate detection collapses identical files into one representative, while your sentences
      quoted inside somebody else's different document survive as separate examples. Being the
      snippet people paste into their own repos is worth more than any amount of promotion.
    </p>

    <h3 id="memorization">Verbatim recall proves inclusion; silence proves nothing</h3>
    <p>
      To test membership we give a model 55 words of your page and measure the longest exact
      continuation it produces, alongside a positive control (the MIT license, which must fire) and a
      fabricated passage (which must not). A long exact run is strong evidence: text cannot be
      reproduced unless it was seen.
    </p>
    <p>
      A null result is weak evidence in the other direction. Models memorize a fraction of what they
      read, prose gets paraphrased rather than copied, and two pages in the same repo can behave
      differently. Supabase's Auth guide reproduces a 23-word run while its Row Level Security guide,
      in the same repository, shows nothing. Read "no model reproduced it" as "we found no proof",
      never as "it is absent".
    </p>
    <p class="learn__note">
      A short run is not the same claim as a long one. Nine words of "returns 204 No Content on
      success" is phrasing thousands of API docs share, so we report it as a hint rather than a
      finding.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- 3. retrieval --------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <section id="retrieval">
    <h2>Retrieval: what an agent can fetch while it answers</h2>

    <p>
      This is the half you can still change. When someone asks Claude or ChatGPT about your product
      today, three things can happen: the model answers from memory, a search index supplies a
      summary, or an agent fetches your URL live. The last two are governed by your server, and they
      are governed <em>per crawler</em>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="crawlers">Each lab runs several crawlers, and robots.txt treats them separately</h3>
    <p>
      Blocking "AI bots" is not one decision. The same company runs a training crawler, a search
      indexer and a user-triggered fetcher, and they are separate user agents with separate effects.
    </p>

    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>User agent</th><th>Run by</th><th>What it feeds</th><th>Blocking it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>CCBot</code></td><td>Common Crawl</td>
            <td>Open web corpora, and the labs that build on them</td>
            <td>You leave the open corpora that most public datasets start from</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>ClaudeBot</code></td><td>Anthropic</td><td>Model training</td>
            <td>Your content is not collected for training</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Claude-SearchBot</code></td><td>Anthropic</td><td>Search result quality</td>
            <td>You are less likely to be surfaced in Claude's search</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Claude-User</code></td><td>Anthropic</td><td>A user asking Claude to read a page</td>
            <td>Claude cannot open your page when a user asks it to</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>GPTBot</code></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>Model training</td>
            <td>Your content is not collected for training</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>OAI-SearchBot</code></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>ChatGPT search results</td>
            <td>You are not surfaced in ChatGPT's search</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>ChatGPT-User</code></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>A user asking ChatGPT to visit a page</td>
            <td>OpenAI documents that robots.txt rules may not apply to this user-initiated fetch</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Google-Extended</code></td><td>Google</td>
            <td>Gemini training and grounding</td>
            <td>Gemini stops using you. Google documents that Search inclusion and ranking are unaffected</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Googlebot</code></td><td>Google</td><td>Search, and the answers built on it</td>
            <td>You leave Google Search</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="learn__note">
      Sources:
      <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/8896518-does-anthropic-crawl-data-from-the-web-and-how-can-site-owners-block-the-crawler" rel="noopener">Anthropic</a>,
      <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/bots" rel="noopener">OpenAI</a> and
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/google-common-crawlers" rel="noopener">Google</a>
      each publish their crawler list. All of them state their crawlers honour robots.txt, with
      OpenAI noting that user-initiated fetches are treated differently.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our <code>robots.txt</code> check reports the training crawlers, because that is the gate that
      is permanent. A page you exclude from training today can still be fetched live tomorrow, and a
      page you allow into training cannot be taken back out.
    </p>

    <h3 id="reachable">A crawler that is allowed can still be blocked at the edge</h3>
    <p>
      <code>robots.txt</code> is a request. Your CDN, WAF or bot-management rule is an enforcement
      point, and the two disagree more often than teams expect. We fetch your page with a crawler
      user agent and report what actually comes back, because "allowed in robots.txt" and "returns
      200 to a crawler" are separate facts.
    </p>

    <h3 id="clean-text">Agents read what you serve, and rendered HTML costs them</h3>
    <p>
      An agent that fetches a documentation page gets your markup: navigation, cookie banners,
      component wrappers, syntax-highlighting spans. It then spends tokens and attention recovering
      the prose you started from. Serving the source text directly removes that step.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two mechanisms do this, and we check for both. <strong>Content negotiation</strong> means
      returning <code>text/markdown</code> when a client sends <code>Accept: text/markdown</code>.
      A <strong>markdown twin</strong> means the same page is also available at a <code>.md</code>
      URL. Neither is required by any lab. Both make the difference between an agent reading your
      sentences and an agent parsing your theme.
    </p>

    <h3 id="sitemap">Crawl budget decides which of your pages exist to be found</h3>
    <p>
      Crawlers reach deep pages far less often without a <code>sitemap.xml</code>, and the pages they
      miss are new, deep and weakly linked. This is the one place where classic crawl hygiene
      transfers directly: ship a sitemap, redirect moved URLs with 301, reduce click depth from the
      homepage, and earn links to the pages you care about.
    </p>

    <h3 id="llms-txt">llms.txt is widely adopted and used by nobody</h3>
    <p>
      <code>llms.txt</code> proposes a curated index of your documentation for LLMs. It has been
      adopted broadly, and
      <a href="https://www.seroundtable.com/google-does-not-endorse-llms-txt-40789.html" rel="noopener">no
      major AI system uses it</a> for training or inference. We report it as informational: present
      or absent, never as a win. It costs little, and it is not the reason a model does or does not
      know you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- 4. outcomes ---------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <section id="outcomes">
    <h2>What the four outcomes mean for you</h2>

    <p>
      Cross the two questions and you get four situations. Each one has a different failure mode and
      a different next move.
    </p>

    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table learn__table--outcomes">
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th scope="col">Retrievable today</th><th scope="col">Not retrievable</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">In the training data</th>
            <td>
              <strong>Your idioms are the default.</strong> The model answers about you without
              being asked to look, and an agent can check the current details when it matters. The
              work here is maintenance: keep the canonical snippet yours, and keep the fetchable
              copy current so the live answer corrects the remembered one.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>The model quotes a version you have moved past.</strong> This is the worst
              failure mode, because it is invisible to you and confident to the user. Nothing can be
              removed from a shipped corpus, so the fix is to make the current page reachable and
              cleanly readable, so live fetches override stale memory.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Not in the training data</th>
            <td>
              <strong>You exist only when someone points at you.</strong> Answers about your product
              are assembled from whatever else the model read: competitors, Stack Overflow, an old
              tutorial. Getting the docs source into a public repo is the highest-value change,
              because it is the channel with no prose quality filter.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>You are absent from both systems.</strong> Whatever the model says about you
              comes from other people's writing about you. Start with collection: a public repo for
              the training side, and crawlability for the retrieval side.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="learn__note">
      One correction that saves a lot of wasted effort: these outcomes are not a funnel, and the
      quality filter is not a quality judgement of your writing. A reference page scoring 1.6 is not
      badly written. It is the wrong genre for a classifier tuned on school material, and the code
      channel exists precisely to carry it.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a class="learn__cta" href="/test">Check a page &rarr;</a>
      <a class="learn__cta learn__cta--quiet" href="/guide">Read the measurements and sources &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- 5. glossary ---------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <section id="glossary">
    <h2>Glossary</h2>

    <dl class="learn__glossary">
      <dt id="common-crawl">Common Crawl</dt>
      <dd>
        A non-profit that crawls the web and publishes the result. Most open web corpora start from
        it. It samples by domain centrality and never copies a domain in full, so presence in it is a
        real measurement rather than a formality. Our check asks whether your exact URL appears in
        the most recent monthly index.
      </dd>

      <dt id="fineweb-edu">FineWeb and FineWeb-Edu</dt>
      <dd>
        FineWeb is an open web corpus built from Common Crawl. FineWeb-Edu is the subset kept by an
        educational-quality classifier, which is
        <a href="https://huggingface.co/HuggingFaceFW/fineweb-edu-classifier" rel="noopener">published
        openly</a>. We score pages with it because it is the only filter in this pipeline anyone can
        actually run. It is a proxy for what a modern web pipeline keeps, not the filter any specific
        lab used.
      </dd>

      <dt id="the-stack">The Stack v2 and v3</dt>
      <dd>
        Open code corpora used to train code models, built by the BigCode project. v2 was built from
        the Software Heritage archive rather than by crawling GitHub directly, and it keeps Markdown
        alongside source files: about 254 GB of Markdown in v1. Its license policy is the one that
        matters in practice: <code>permissive</code> and <code>no_license</code> are kept,
        <code>non_permissive</code> is dropped. v1's stricter rule, which excluded unlicensed files,
        is what most advice still describes.
      </dd>

      <dt id="swh">Software Heritage</dt>
      <dd>
        A long-term archive of public source code. It is the collection step for The Stack v2 and v3.
        Archival is not automatic: repositories are picked up through listers and explicit save
        requests, so a popular permissive repo can simply be absent. Our check asks the archive
        whether it holds a full visit of your repository.
      </dd>

      <dt id="ccbot">CCBot</dt>
      <dd>
        Common Crawl's crawler. Blocking it in <code>robots.txt</code> removes you from future
        crawls, and therefore from the corpora built on them. It is not operated by any AI lab.
      </dd>

      <dt id="memorization-probe">Memorization probe</dt>
      <dd>
        Our test for whether a model has seen a passage: show it the first 55 words and measure the
        longest exact continuation. We run several passages against several models, plus two
        controls. The MIT license must fire and an invented passage must not; if the controls
        misbehave, no result in that run is interpretable.
      </dd>

      <dt id="markdown-twin">Markdown twin</dt>
      <dd>
        The same page served as Markdown at a parallel URL, usually by appending <code>.md</code>. It
        gives an agent the source text instead of the rendered theme. Related but distinct from
        content negotiation, which serves Markdown at the same URL when the client asks for it.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <%# --- 6. limits ------------------------------------------------------------------------ %>
  <section id="limits">
    <h2>What this cannot tell you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>These are open proxies, not any lab's filter.</strong> Common Crawl, FineWeb-Edu, The
        Stack and Software Heritage are public and documented. Anthropic, OpenAI and Google do not
        disclose their pipelines. We measure what a representative open pipeline would do.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Quality scores are single runs on the first ~512 tokens.</strong> Differences under
        0.3 are noise.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A negative memorization result is weak evidence.</strong> It says we found no proof,
        which is a much smaller claim than absence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Inclusion is not influence.</strong> Text that reached a corpus is still
        deduplicated, weighted and mixed with everything else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rules are versioned.</strong> Any claim about licenses has to name the corpus
        version, because The Stack v1 and v2 disagree about unlicensed code.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="learn__meta">
      Every number on this page is reproducible from <code>scripts/</code> and <code>data/</code> in
      <a href="https://github.com/irinanazarova/ruby-scorecard" rel="noopener">the repo</a>, and the
      full evidence with sources is in <a href="/guide">the guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
